#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fpga/targets/@BOARD@/@BLOCK@.timing.xdc  --  WHAT THIS DESIGN PROMISES ABOUT
#                                              TIME
#
# Scaffolded into @PROJECT@ by `fpga-flow-init` on @DATE@.
# Named by XDC_TIMING in fpga/design.mk. READ IN IMPLEMENTATION ONLY -
# the engine sets USED_IN_SYNTHESIS false on this file.
#
# WHAT BELONGS IN THIS FILE
#   The clock definitions this design's timing is closed against, the IO
#   delays, the clock groups, and the exceptions - false paths, multicycles,
#   max/min delays.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT
#   * pins, IO standards, electrical properties  ->  @BLOCK@.pins.xdc
#   * DRC severities and configuration properties  ->  @BLOCK@.drc.xdc
#   * a DRC WAIVER. It cannot be in an XDC at all - see the trap in
#     @BLOCK@.drc.xdc and XDC_POST_ROUTE in design.mk.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE IS IMPLEMENTATION-ONLY, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A DETAIL ──────
#
# Constraints read during synthesis are APPLIED during synthesis. Three
# consequences, and the first two are the ones that bite:
#
#   1. AN EXCEPTION READ IN SYNTHESIS CHANGES WHAT SYNTHESIS BUILDS. A
#      `set_false_path` tells the tool not to work on a path. If the exception
#      is wrong, synthesis will not have built the logic that could have closed
#      it, and implementation cannot recover what was never there.
#
#   2. THE OBJECT NAMES DO NOT MEAN THE SAME THING IN BOTH PASSES. An exception
#      anchored to a pin, a cell or a generated clock names something that
#      exists after elaboration and mapping. The same line read BEFORE that
#      matches a different set - often the empty set - and Vivado drops a
#      constraint that matches nothing WITHOUT AN ERROR. So the same file
#      quietly means two things and no log distinguishes them.
#
#   3. A synthesis timing report is then unmistakably not a signoff number,
#      because synthesis was not given the constraints. That is a feature: it
#      removes a number people quote.
#
# The engine decides this from WHICH VARIABLE NAMED THE FILE. Moving a clock
# definition into the pins file does not keep it out of synthesis - it puts it
# in.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRAP: THE PERIOD HERE IS THE OSCILLATOR'S, NOT SYS_CLK_FREQ_HZ.
#
#   `create_clock` on a PORT describes the waveform ARRIVING AT THE PIN. That
#   is the board's oscillator - `oscillator_hz` in the board pack.
#
#   SYS_CLK_FREQ_HZ in design.mk is the SYSTEM clock: what the design runs at
#   after the MMCM/PLL, and it is also compiled into the firmware as
#   -DNANOSOC_SYS_CLK_FREQ_HZ. THEY ARE TWO DIFFERENT NUMBERS and only one of
#   them belongs on the line below.
#
#   Do NOT `create_clock` the MMCM outputs. The tool derives generated clocks
#   from the primitive's own settings, and a hand-written `create_clock` on a
#   generated clock's net REPLACES that derivation with your arithmetic -
#   silently, and usually with the multiply/divide of the day the file was
#   written. `create_generated_clock` belongs only where the tool genuinely
#   cannot derive the relationship (a clock divided in the fabric, a clock
#   crossing a black box).
#
#   A build whose firmware and fabric disagree about the system clock BOOTS. It
#   comes up, runs, and gets every baud rate and every timer wrong by the ratio
#   between them - so the UART prints garbage at the rate you expected and the
#   symptom reads as a serial wiring fault.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRAP: `set_clock_groups -asynchronous` IS A PROMISE, NOT AN ANALYSIS.
#
#   It tells the tool to STOP TIMING the paths between two domains. It does not
#   check that a synchroniser exists, that the crossing is on a bus that needs
#   one, or that the handshake is sound. Every crossing it covers becomes
#   invisible to every timing report for the rest of the design's life.
#
#   That is the right constraint for a genuinely asynchronous crossing WITH a
#   synchroniser, and it is a way to hide a real defect for months otherwise.
#   Write the diagnosis beside each one: which two domains, why they are
#   asynchronous, and where the synchroniser is. If you cannot name the
#   synchroniser, you are not describing a crossing - you are silencing one.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ── THE EXCEPTION PRIORITY LADDER ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   set_false_path  >  set_max_delay / set_min_delay  >  set_multicycle_path
#
# A stronger exception silently outranks a weaker one on the same path, so a
# `set_multicycle_path` under an overlapping `set_false_path` does nothing at
# all and reports nothing at all. When two exceptions overlap, only the
# strongest is in force; write them so they do not overlap, or write down which
# one you meant to win.
#
# ── EVERY .xdc HERE MUST BE NAMED BY A VARIABLE IN design.mk ────────────────
#
# An .xdc in this directory that no variable names is an ERROR from
# `make check`, deliberately: the flow this replaces discovered constraints by
# a glob and silently ignored anything named differently. Add XDC_EXTRA (an
# ordered list) or XDC_OPTIONAL (a "COND:path" list) rather than dropping a
# file in and hoping.
#
# Copyright (C) 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


#### 1. THE PRIMARY CLOCK ######################################################
#
# The period is in NANOSECONDS and it is the OSCILLATOR's - read the trap
# above. The port is the same one @BLOCK@.pins.xdc gave a package pin to.

create_clock -name sys_clk -period <<FILL IN: the OSCILLATOR period in ns, from oscillator_hz in the board pack>> [get_ports <<FILL IN: the clock port on TOP>>]


#### 2. GENERATED AND SECONDARY CLOCKS #########################################
#
# Usually NOTHING GOES HERE. The tool derives MMCM/PLL outputs by itself, and
# every hand-written definition of a derived clock is arithmetic that can drift
# from the primitive that actually generates it.
#
# Only for a clock the tool cannot derive - divided in the fabric, or arriving
# through something it cannot see through:
#
# create_generated_clock -name <name> -source [get_pins <src pin>] -divide_by <n> [get_pins <dest pin>]


#### 3. CLOCK RELATIONSHIPS ####################################################
#
# Read the -asynchronous trap above before uncommenting anything here, and
# write the diagnosis on the line: which two domains, why they are unrelated,
# and WHERE THE SYNCHRONISER IS.
#
# set_clock_groups -asynchronous \
#     -group [get_clocks <clock a>] \
#     -group [get_clocks <clock b>]


#### 4. IO TIMING ##############################################################
#
# AN UNCONSTRAINED PORT IS NOT A FAST PORT, IT IS AN UNTIMED ONE. With no
# set_input_delay/set_output_delay the tool has no requirement to meet, reports
# no violation, and the interface either works on the bench or does not.
#
# The numbers are properties of what is on the other side of the pin - the
# device's datasheet, the PCB trace, the connector - so they belong to this
# target, not to the design.
#
# set_input_delay  -clock <clock> -max <ns> [get_ports <port>]
# set_input_delay  -clock <clock> -min <ns> [get_ports <port>]
# set_output_delay -clock <clock> -max <ns> [get_ports <port>]
# set_output_delay -clock <clock> -min <ns> [get_ports <port>]
#
# For a port that genuinely has no timing relationship - a button, an LED, a
# jumper - say so explicitly rather than leaving it unconstrained by omission.
# The difference matters to the next reader:
#
# set_false_path -from [get_ports <the asynchronous input>]
# set_false_path -to   [get_ports <the indicator output>]


#### 5. EXCEPTIONS #############################################################
#
# Each one is a claim about the design that the tool cannot verify. Write the
# claim next to it, with the date and the run it was measured on. An exception
# with no reason beside it is the first thing the next person deletes to make a
# number move, and the second thing they put back when the board stops working.
#
# set_false_path     -from [get_cells <a>] -to [get_cells <b>]     ;# why
# set_multicycle_path -setup <n> -from [get_cells <a>] -to [get_cells <b>]
# set_multicycle_path -hold  [expr {<n> - 1}] -from [get_cells <a>] -to [get_cells <b>]
#
# THE HOLD MULTICYCLE IS NOT OPTIONAL. A `-setup N` on its own moves the setup
# check out by N cycles and LEAVES THE HOLD CHECK WHERE IT WAS, which is one
# cycle after a launch that no longer happens there. The pair is the
# constraint; half of it is a different constraint that happens to share a
# name.

